Elevated fluoride (F-) in groundwater primarily arises from granitic rocks containing fluoride-rich minerals such as fluorite (CaF₂), fluorapatite (Ca₅(PO₄)₃F), biotite [K(Mg,Fe)₃AlSi₃O₁₀(F,OH)₂], hornblende [Ca₂(Mg,Fe)₅Si₈O₂₂(OH,F)₂], and feldspars21. Granite typically contains 0.03–0.10% fluoride, with biotite and hornblende reaching up to 1.1% and 2.9%, respectively44. While caliche-bearing sands and laterites provide local contributions, Balasore’s igneous–metamorphic basement (granites, gneisses, schists) serves as the primary, region-wide fluoride reservoir. Fluorapatite causes a slow, continuous fluoride release, whereas fluorite dissolution under alkaline, Ca2+-poor conditions produces transient fluoride spikes creating F- hotspots. Biotite and amphiboles further supply moderate fluoride fluxes through continuous silicate weathering, influenced by aquifer pH, calcium availability, and groundwater residence time. Although CaF₂ has a low solubility (Kₛₚ ≈ 10⁻11), its dissolution can be enhanced in bicarbonate-rich and alkaline groundwater. The following reactions illustrate F- mobilization under such conditions;15\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
